id: pm-q2n4vd
title: "The project's own agent-facing documentation and skills instruct deprecated command spellings, so the alias-usage signal that decides when a spelling can be retired is manufactured by the instructions rather than observed from callers"
description: "Measured 2026-08-30. PM_COMMAND_ALIAS_CONTRACTS declares 23 executable command aliases, of which 10 carry lifecycle deprecated: list-all, list-draft, list-open, list-in-progress, list-blocked, list-closed, list-canceled, extension, install and upgrade. Each remains executable, prints a migration hint on stderr, and is intentionally absent from help and completion discovery.\n\nTwenty-seven fenced command-position invocations across nineteen agent-facing markdown files instructed one of those ten spellings. The distribution is what makes it structural rather than cosmetic. AGENTS.md, the file the operating rules name as the low-context entrypoint, told every agent to run pm install guide-shell --project. Four of the five .agents/skills SKILL.md files repeated it, as did docs/AGENT_GUIDE.md, docs/README.md, docs/ONBOARDING.md and .agents/skills/HARNESS_COMPATIBILITY.md. docs/COMMANDS.md, the canonical command reference, additionally asserted in prose that pm install and pm package are the preferred package-first workflow, which contradicts the alias table that marks install as deprecated. One skill instructed pm list-open, whose canonical form is pm list --status open.\n\nTwo consequences compound. Every such invocation emits a deprecation hint line the agent then has to read and discard, which is pure token cost on the highest-frequency paths. More seriously, pm-0mox proposes alias-usage counters precisely so grammar-freeze removals are evidence-based; if the project's own instructions are the dominant source of alias invocations, the counter measures the documentation rather than the installed base, and the removal decision it is supposed to inform is made from a signal the project generated for itself.\n\nThe docs and skills gate could not catch this because it enforced the presence of the deprecated spelling: its required routing marker was the literal string pm install guide-shell, so a skill written in the canonical spelling failed the gate and a skill written in the deprecated spelling passed. The enforcement pointed the wrong way."
type: Issue
status: closed
priority: 1
tags[6]: agent-ux,"area:cli","area:docs",denominator,governance,token-efficiency
